<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Calculate Gross Margin Percentage</h2>
    <p>Gross margin measures the percentage of total revenue remaining after deducting direct production expenses or software hosting COGS. High gross margins allow businesses to re-invest heavily in customer acquisition, product research, and enterprise expansion.</p>
    <p>Calculating gross profit margin requires two fundamental inputs:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Total Revenue:</strong> The total gross sales earnings generated over a given financial period.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost of Goods Sold (COGS):</strong> Direct variable expenses such as cloud hosting, server infrastructure, payment gateway processing fees, third-party API costs, and customer support payroll.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>The Gross Margin Formula</h2>
    <p>Gross Profit Margin is calculated by subtracting COGS from total revenue and dividing by total revenue:</p>
    <p><code>Gross Margin % = [(Total Revenue - COGS) / Total Revenue] x 100</code></p>
    <p>For example, if Total Revenue is $100,000 and COGS is $20,000: <code>Gross Profit = $100,000 - $20,000 = $80,000.00</code>. Margin percentage equals <code>($80,000 / $100,000) x 100 = 80.00%</code>.</p>
    
    <h3>Markup vs Gross Margin Formula</h3>
    <p>While gross margin measures profit relative to selling price, markup measures profit relative to cost:</p>
    <p><code>Markup % = (Gross Profit / COGS) x 100</code></p>
    <p>With $80,000 gross profit and $20,000 COGS, markup equals <code>($80,000 / $20,000) x 100 = 400.00%</code>.</p>
</section>

